+quotation classes sit in Type. The showcase's own button drives the controller and announces the
+selected mode, and its entry constructs a `Delegate` instance beside the showcase. The application
+barrel is a workspace implementation surface and is outside this guide's published API tables.
+
+That placement is what the capture registry addresses a frame by: every registered scenario names
+the specimen or the region that is its subject, and § Tests gives the filename law built on it, so a
+shipped key no region renders has no subject to register and no frame to be read from.
 
 Scaffold mandates the Vue toolchain for an `app/browser` environment, and this shell declares no
 component in it: the shell is framework-free by design, so the showcase drives the published
@@ -1105,6 +1108,70 @@ The application proofs drive the shell through its interface; see
 [typography class specimens](../tests/app/browser/sections/TypeSection.test.ts),
 [media class specimens](../tests/app/browser/sections/MediaSection.test.ts), and
 [showcase journeys](../tests/app/browser/integration.test.ts).
+
+The journey run also writes the capture portfolio an appearance round is ruled on. Each frame is
+named `<scenario>--<theme>-<viewport>.png`: the stem names the scenario alone, and the token after
+the separator is the variant that rendered it, which is its theme and its viewport together. A run
+photographs only the mode its own variant names, so the theme a filename claims and the theme its
+frame shows are one reading, and no token names the project that shot the frame.
+
+A scenario's stem is its own subject's stem, and a driven state follows that stem: the `Primary`
+specimen held under the pointer is `primary-active`. A subject is a specimen one of the showcase
+tables declares, or the region a page frame reads the surface through. The `tests/setup.ts` module
+declares that grammar as the types the registry rows are written in, so a scenario spelling a
+project suffix or a mode token fails the typecheck at the row declaring it. The registry proof in
+[showcase journeys](../tests/app/browser/integration.test.ts) holds every registered subject against
+those tables and against the rendered surface, so a scenario whose subject the showcase stops
+rendering reddens rather than photographing something else under the same name.
+
+The stem is what lets this portfolio be sorted against a counterpart portfolio by subject rather
+than by hand. The rule comes from the Bootstrap 5.3.8 portfolio the CL13 round shot of the same
+markup, whose stems match that registry's specimen names. Nothing here pairs the two directories:
+no listing of that portfolio is retained in this repository, so the appearance round that reads one
+pairs the rows and rules on the alignment.
+
+| Veneer stem        | Subject                                               |
+| ------------------ | ----------------------------------------------------- |
+| `capped-container` | the `Capped container` specimen's own container       |
+| `numbered-columns` | the `Numbered columns` specimen's own row             |
+| `base`             | the `Base` table specimen                             |
+| `role-links`       | the `Role links` specimen, every anchor it renders    |
+| `showcase`         | the `Showcase` region, as a frame of the resting page |
+| `primary-focus`    | the `Primary` specimen reached by keyboard traversal  |
+| `primary-hover`    | the `Primary` specimen under the pointer              |
+| `primary-active`   | the `Primary` specimen held under the pointer         |
+| `toggle-pressed`   | the `Toggle` specimen driven to its pressed state     |
+
+An element frame carries its whole specimen and the background that specimen renders against, and
+the element the scenario is about is the frame's declared region. A crop of that element alone
+carries one role link and none of the paint behind it, which is why the frame is the specimen and
+the region is the reading. The written frame is read back through the installed `readFrame` function
+for its size and the color its bottom row paints, and its declared region is read for the fraction
+of pixels differing from that region's first pixel; a region painting one color is refused as a
+blank.
+
+The guard's population is the frames on disk under `tmp/capture/states`, discovered from the
+registry's own expansion and read back whether or not this run wrote a file, so an ordinary journey
+run guards the frames an earlier capture run left. Each frame is paired with the region its scenario
+declared while the shot was staged, because the written file carries no box and the handed-back
+document no longer holds one. A directory holding no frame is reported as that, and held to the
+directory rather than passed over as a clean sweep.
+
+One accessibility artifact covers one subject and one variant, and it is named
+`<subject>--<theme>-<viewport>-accessibility.txt`. Its stem is the subject's where a frame's stem is
+the scenario's, so the Button scenarios' frames share the `primary` and the `toggle` artifact. Each
+artifact names its subject, its variant, and the state its reading was taken in, which is `rest`:
+the reading is taken on arrival while a driven frame shows focus, the pointer, or a press. It
+carries that subject's accessible tree and its keyboard focus order, both read through a scope
+enclosing the subject, so a subject that is itself a control records its own role and name rather
+than reporting itself unreachable. The per-variant manifest `tmp/capture/<variant>.txt` keeps the
+whole page's tree, the focus inventory, the interaction log, and every reading the journeys took.
+
+The two portfolios read side by side within one stated limit: a counterpart frame is shot on a blank
+page carrying the specimen alone, and a Veneer frame is a lift out of the mounted showcase. The
+hosts are not the same document, so inherited context is not proven identical, and a difference
+between the sides is bounded by that before it is read as a cascade difference.
+
